<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nectanebo I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 362 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Nakhtnebef</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nectanebo I was the founder of the Thirtieth Dynasty, the last native dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, reigning from 380 to 362 BCE. A general from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebennytos">Sebennytos</a> in the Delta, he deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepherites-ii">Nepherites II</a> of the Twenty-ninth Dynasty. In 373 BCE he repelled a Persian invasion led by Pharnabazus and the Athenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iphicrates">Iphicrates</a>, helped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> flood, and he was a great temple builder, with works at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> and elsewhere.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nectanebo-i.jpg" alt="Nectanebo I" width="320" height="367" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marco Prins · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nectanebo_I_with_khepresh_crown.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nectanebo's Egyptian personal name was Nakhtnebef, meaning the strong one of his lord. Greek sources rendered it Nectanebis. He was an army general from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebennytos">Sebennytos</a>, the son of an important military officer named Djedhor and of a lady whose name is only partly recorded. A stele found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermopolis">Hermopolis</a> provides some evidence that he came to power by overthrowing, and possibly killing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepherites-ii">Nepherites II</a>, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the 29th Dynasty. It has been suggested that the Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chabrias">Chabrias</a> helped him in the coup. He held his coronation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a> and Memphis and moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendes">Mendes</a> to Sebennytos. He called Nepherites II inept and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakor">Achoris</a> a usurper, but seems to have thought better of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepherites-i">Nepherites I</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Nectanebo was a great builder and restorer, to an extent not seen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for centuries. On the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a> he began the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> by erecting its vestibule, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> he began the First Pylon in the precinct of Amun-Re. The earliest known mammisi, at Dendera, is believed to be his. He supported the cult of sacred animals, as archaeological finds at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermopolis">Hermopolis</a>, Saft el-Hinna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendes">Mendes</a> show, and he was generous to the priesthood. A decree of his first year, found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naucratis">Naucratis</a>, assigned 10 per cent of the taxes on imports and local production there to the temple of Neith at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a>.</p><p>The Achaemenid king Artaxerxes II, who still counted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> a rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a>, prepared for six years and sent a great army under the Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iphicrates">Iphicrates</a> and the Persian Pharnabazus. Nectanebo fortified the Pelusiac branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, which forced the enemy fleet up the less defended Mendesian branch. Mutual distrust between Iphicrates and Pharnabazus kept them from reaching Memphis, and the annual Nile flood and the resolve of the defenders turned apparent defeat into complete victory. From 368 BCE many western satrapies rebelled against Artaxerxes, and Nectanebo gave financial support to the rebels and renewed ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nectanebo died in the 19th year of his rule. His tomb, sarcophagus and mummy have never been found. Late in his reign, probably to remedy the dynastic problems of his predecessors, he restored the old practice of co-regency and associated his son Teos with the throne. Soon after Teos came to power, however, his brother Tjahapimu betrayed him and put his own son Nakhthorheb, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-ii">Nectanebo II</a>, on the throne. Nectanebo I had founded the last native dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and his building programme gave later temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> their beginnings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nectanebo I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Nectanebo I</b>, a general from Sebennytos in the Delta, seized power from the Twenty-ninth Dynasty of Egypt in 380 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/thirtieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Thirtieth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>In 380 BCE <b>Nectanebo I</b>, a general from Sebennytos, deposed Nepherites II and founded the Thirtieth Dynasty of Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-ninth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-ninth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
